Simple laceration from sharp projecting object snag to lower leg
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Linear dermo-epidermal defect, Easily compressible bleeding, Stationary snag mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-018
Reason for Visit: Acute bleeding leg wound requiring urgent assessment for deep tissue damage, retained foreign body, and definitive closure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, bleeding laceration after walking into a sharp object. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to achieve hemostasis, rule out fascial or tendinous disruption, and perform primary closure within the golden window to prevent secondary infection before standard daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I snagged my leg on a sharp piece of metal and it sliced the skin open; it keeps bleeding through the bandages.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a clear history of walking into a stationary sharp metal projection. This resulted in a linear break in the epidermis and dermis extending into the superficial subcutaneous fat. Bleeding from the wound edges was brisk but easily controlled with localized direct pressure. The patient experiences mild to moderate stinging pain localized strictly to the wound site, with minimal to no surrounding soft tissue swelling or crush artifact. The patient noted minor superficial debris at the wound margin initially.
Functional Status: Independent ambulation, though slightly guarded.
Social/Safety Concerns: Protruding metal object in the yard requires removal to prevent recurrent injury.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, leg elevated on a chair.
Physical Exam: Lower limb examination reveals a 4cm linear dermo-epidermal defect on the anterior lower leg. Visible subcutaneous adipose tissue is noted without deep exposure of muscular fascia. The easily compressible bleeding confirms absence of pulsatile arterial hemorrhage. Bedside clinical exploration under local anesthesia confirms the wound depth and absence of retained foreign bodies. Intact distal neurovascular function is verified with strong palpable pedal pulses, normal capillary refill, and intact sensation to light touch distal to the wound.
Environmental Observations: Sharp metal garden edging identified as the cause; patient advised to cap or remove it.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple laceration to lower leg - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep fascial laceration/muscle belly injury - Ruled out due to: Direct visual exploration confirming an intact fascial layer at the base of the wound.

Arterial laceration - Ruled out due to: Easily controlled, non-spurting venous/capillary bleeding with strong distal pulses preserved.

Retained foreign body - Ruled out due to: Thorough wound exploration and irrigation revealing a clean wound bed devoid of debris.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The mechanism of a snag injury on the lower leg carries a risk for deeper structural damage or retained foreign material that is easily missed without formal exploration. Bedside evaluation under local anesthetic definitively ruled out deep space involvement and foreign bodies, confirming an isolated simple laceration. Urgent after-hours assessment and primary closure were clinically mandated to prevent heavy environmental bacterial colonization, stop persistent bleeding, and facilitate optimal functional recovery, which would be compromised by delaying closure until the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered local anesthesia (1% Lidocaine with Epinephrine). Performed high-pressure syringe irrigation with 500mL sterile saline. Closed with simple interrupted non-absorbable sutures. Tetanus booster administered on site.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the leg elevated whenever seated to reduce local venous pressure. This directly reduces throbbing pain and prevents tension on the new suture line, decreasing the risk of the wound bursting open.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Simple non-adherent dressings.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 10-14 days for suture removal.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or your GP if the wound becomes increasingly red, hot to the touch, begins to throb intensely, or discharges yellow/green pus.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Wounds on the lower leg heal more slowly due to reduced circulation and higher dependency pressure compared to the upper body.
Clinical Course: The wound will be mildly sore for 2-3 days. Sutures must remain in place for up to 14 days to ensure adequate tensile strength before removal.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention if red streaks appear traveling up your leg, or if the skin around the stitches becomes dark, swollen, and extremely painful.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Sutures act as mechanical bridges, holding the skin edges perfectly together so the body can quickly lay down new collagen to seal the gap.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing clean and dry for 48 hours. Elevate your leg on a footstool when watching TV. Do not pick at the scabs forming around the stitches.

